//:: cases TC_LK_2_UnsatSymptomCaught
//:: tools silicon
//:: verdict Fail

// TC-LK-2: The SYMPTOM of an unsatisfiable lock invariant is caught by the
// existing appendCheck(Lock) in DetectDeadCode, but the ROOT CAUSE is not.
//
// The invariant (val > 5 AND val < 3) is always false.  After 'lock this',
// EncodeIntrinsicLock does:
//   inhale Perm(lockInv, write)          -- acquire the predicate
//   unfold lockInv                        -- expand it: val>5 ** val<3 → false
//   inhale Perm(held, write)             -- acquire held token
// The state is now contradictory (false).  The appendCheck(Lock) that
// DetectDeadCode inserts right after 'lock this' is:
//   refute false  →  assert false (expected to fail)
// From a contradictory state, 'assert false' passes, the expected error is NOT
// tripped, RefuteFailed fires → DeadBranch("code after locking this").
//
// Gap: this fires once per method that uses 'lock this', all reporting the
// same root cause.  A CheckLockInvariantSatisfiability pass (analogous to
// CheckLoopInvariantSatisfiability) would fire LockInvariantUnsatisfiable
// once at the CLASS level using:
//   FramedProof(pre=true, body={ Inhale(lockInv); refute false }, post=true)
// before any method is verified.
lock_invariant Perm(val, write) ** val > 5 ** val < 3;
class UnsatInvClass {
    int val;

    requires committed(this);
    void method_a() {
        lock this;
        // State: false (invariant is unsat).  appendCheck inserts refute false here.
        // 'assert false' passes from contradictory state → DeadBranch fires.
        if (val > 0) { }  // dead — but error says "dead after locking", not "invariant unsat"
        unlock this;
    }

    // The same root cause fires a second separate DeadBranch in method_b.
    // A class-level check would report this once and stop here.
    requires committed(this);
    void method_b() {
        lock this;
        if (val < 10) { }  // dead — same unsat invariant, same DeadBranch error again
        unlock this;
    }
}
